## ABSTRACT

This dissertation proposes, formalizes, and technically specifies the CosmoCat Feline Extraterrestrial Mobility and Survival Suit (FEMSS) — the first rigorous engineering and mathematical framework for the sustained habitability of domestic cats (*Felis catus*) in extraterrestrial environments. We resume the interrupted scientific lineage of Félicette (France, 1963), the only cat to have reached space, and extend it from a brief suborbital measurement to a formal multi-year mission architecture.

The work is organized around four original scientific contributions. First, we derive the **Feline Vestibular Compensation Model (FVCM)**, a geometric mechanics framework grounded in the Kane-Scher (1969) two-cylinder model and Montgomery's (1993) gauge-theoretic reformulation on the SO(3) principal bundle, which we extend to partial gravity environments g ∈ [0, 9.81] m/s² and augment with a suit-embedded haptic correction layer. Second, we specify a five-layer **Multifunctional Nanocomposite Suit Architecture** with material-specific thermal conductivity, radiation attenuation, and mechanical compliance bounds derived from current literature on laser-induced graphene textiles and boron nitride-graphene aerogels. Third, we design the **Feline Continuous Autonomic Monitoring System (FCAMS)**, a multimodal edge-AI biosensor platform fusing ECG, respiratory inductance plethysmography, accelerometry, galvanic skin response, and machine vision into a Feline Stress Index (FSI) computed by a 1D-CNN + Transformer architecture under ≤100 mW power budget. Fourth, we introduce the **Feline Adaptation Index (FAI)** — a nonlinear dynamical systems model of the cat's neurophysiological state trajectory under mission parameters, equipped with a Lyapunov stability proof for the adapted regime and a saddle-node bifurcation characterization of the maladaptation threshold. Together, these four contributions constitute the formal backbone of the COSMOCAT program and establish feline extraterrestrial habitability as a tractable scientific discipline.

