## CHAPTER 0 — HISTORICAL PRECEDENT AND SCIENTIFIC LINEAGE

The participation of living organisms in space exploration precedes human spaceflight by more than a decade. Animals in space originally served to test the survivability of spaceflight before human spaceflights were attempted, with a wide variety of non-human animals launched including monkeys, apes, dogs, cats, tortoises, mice, rats, rabbits, fish, frogs, spiders, and insects. [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animals_in_space)

Within this lineage, the feline contribution is singular and underappreciated. Félicette, a stray cat from the streets of Paris, was launched aboard the Véronique AG1 rocket from the Hammaguir test range in Algeria, reaching an altitude of approximately 157 kilometers. The mission's primary objective was to study the effects of microgravity and space travel on biological functions, and Félicette was equipped with electrodes implanted in her brain to measure neural impulses, providing scientists with invaluable data on how spaceflight could affect the nervous system of a living organism. [Sdnews](https://sdnews.com/cats-in-space-a-fanciful-history-of-felines-and-their-role-in-space-exploration/)

Parallel to this orbital history, ground-based feline research proved foundational to human spaceflight training. In the 1950s, US Air Force Aerospace Medical Research Lab researchers placed cats in parabolic flight to analyze the righting reflex under weightlessness, and the successful experiments showed that astronauts could be trained to twist their bodies like cats to turn around in microgravity. [Science Alert](https://www.sciencealert.com/scientists-put-cats-in-microgravity-to-see-what-would-happen)

Since cats rely heavily on their vestibular system to move through their environment, observing their responses in microgravity deepened understanding of how orientation works beyond Earth's gravity, with data on motor functions and reflexive behavior contributing to advances in bioastronautics. [New Space Economy](https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2025/03/05/cosmic-cats-feline-spacefarers/)

The present work asks the question Félicette's mission could not answer: not whether a cat can survive minutes in space, but whether one can *thrive* across years — and what engineering, mathematics, and ethics are required to make that possible.

