## CHAPTER 1 — PROBLEM STATEMENT AND MISSION PARAMETERS

### 1.1 The Human-Feline Companionship Hypothesis

Psychological and social issues affect space crew due to isolation, confinement, and prolonged separation from family and friends — and this becomes especially critical given plans for Martian colonies and space tourism. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214552420300870) The prolonged isolation and confinement that astronauts face in space can increase risks of behavioral issues and psychiatric disorders such as anxiety and depression, impacting a crew member's sleep, morale, and decision making, making ongoing research about how to help crews maintain psychological health essential for the success and safety of future missions. [NASA](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/risk-of-behavioral-changes-and-psychiatric-disorders/)

The companion animal hypothesis holds that the presence of a living, socially responsive creature provides a continuous, low-bandwidth, high-salience countermeasure to isolation-induced dysregulation. Cats are uniquely suited for this role: they are compact (3–6 kg), metabolically efficient, socially responsive without demanding continuous attention, and form cortisol-modulating attachments with humans. No engineering system has yet been designed to support them safely across a multi-year mission.

### 1.2 Target Mission Envelope

We define the following formal mission parameters that all subsystem specifications must satisfy:

**Gravitational range:** g ∈ {0 (transit), 0.165 (Moon), 0.376 (Mars), 1.0 (Earth-analog)} m/s²

**Radiation budget:** Annual whole-body dose ≤ 200 mSv. Bone marrow dose ≤ 150 mSv/year. During a Solar Particle Event (SPE), peak dose rate ≤ 1 mSv/min for ≤ 30 min.

**Thermal environment:** Habitat temperature range 18°C–26°C nominal. Suit inner surface temperature must remain within ±2°C of 38.5°C (feline thermoneutral zone).

**Mission duration:** T_mission ≥ 900 days (Mars conjunction class).

**Suit mass budget:** m_suit ≤ 400 g (≤ 10% of mean feline body mass).

**Power budget:** P_FCAMS ≤ 100 mW continuous. P_haptic ≤ 250 mW peak.

**Behavioral acceptance criterion:** Voluntary suit donning rate ≥ 80% within 90 days of positive reinforcement conditioning.

