## CHAPTER 3 — SUIT ARCHITECTURE: FIVE-LAYER NANOCOMPOSITE SPECIFICATION

### 3.1 System Overview

The FEMSS shell is a conformal, five-layer nanocomposite structure covering the dorsal, lateral, and cranial surfaces of the cat. The ventral surface is partially open to permit grooming behavior. Total areal mass budget: ≤ 200 g/m². For a cat body surface area of approximately 0.2 m², this yields a shell mass of ≤ 40 g — well within the 400 g total suit budget.

### 3.2 Layer 1 — Outer Radiation and Micrometeoroid Shell

**Material:** Boron nitride–graphene hybrid aerogel (BN-GA) with embedded polyethylene microsphere dispersion.

**Radiation physics:** GCR shielding via hydrogen-rich polymer follows the Bethe-Bloch energy loss relation. For a proton of kinetic energy T traversing a material of areal density ρx (g/cm²):

−dT/d(ρx) = (4πe⁴z²NₐZ)/(m_e c² β² A) · [ln(2m_e c²β²γ²/I) − β²]

where I is the mean excitation energy. Polyethylene (CH₂)_n has a hydrogen mass fraction of 14.4% — among the highest of any structural polymer — and an effective stopping power approximately 20% greater than aluminum per unit mass for proton energies in the 100–1000 MeV range relevant to GCR.

**Aerogel specification:** Boron nitride-graphene hybrid aerogels have been tested for deep-space applications, showing superior suppression of both thermal radiation and heat conduction, with the capacity to sustain 10-hour heating-cooling cycles inside a lunar base model. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10476888/) We specify:

- Density: ρ_BN-GA ≤ 5 mg/cm³
- Thermal conductivity: λ ≤ 0.025 W/(m·K)
- Compressive strain tolerance: ε_max ≥ 99% (superelastic)
- Layer thickness: 3 mm → areal mass ≈ 1.5 g/m²

**Micrometeoroid protection:** For particles in the LEO flux distribution (mass m ≤ 10⁻⁶ g, velocity v ≈ 10 km/s), kinetic energy K = ½mv² ≤ 50 mJ. The BN-GA layer with a backing Kevlar weave (50 g/m²) provides impact resistance consistent with ESA multi-layer insulation standards for unmanned spacecraft.

### 3.3 Layer 2 — EMI and Thermal Regulation Aerogel

**Material:** Graphene aerogel / polyethylene glycol (GA-PEG) phase-change composite.

**Thermal performance:** Graphene aerogel composites loaded with polyethylene glycol achieve low thermal conductivity of 0.29 W·m⁻¹·K⁻¹, high latent heat storage of 156.80 J·g⁻¹, and excellent long-term irradiation resistance. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352152X25011697)

Phase-change latent heat buffering: For a 5 g layer of GA-PEG, thermal energy storage capacity:

Q_buffer = m_PCM · L_f = 0.005 · 156.8 = 0.784 kJ

For a cat generating 3 W of metabolic heat during active locomotion, the time to exhaust the buffer:

t_buffer = Q_buffer / P_metabolic = 784 / 3 ≈ 261 seconds ≈ 4.4 minutes

This is sufficient to absorb transient thermal spikes from activity bursts. The baseline thermal regulation mesh handles steady-state heat dissipation.

**EMI shielding:** Layered biomimetic structures as EMI shielding materials enhance effectiveness via multiple reflections and scattering, absorption and dissipation, and lengthening of propagation paths, while maintaining mechanical flexibility. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10837381/) The GA-PEG layer provides SE ≥ 20 dB across 1–18 GHz, protecting the FCAMS electronics from habitat RF environment.

### 3.4 Layer 3 — Biometric E-Textile Sensor Array

**Material:** Laser-induced graphene (LIG) on flexible Kevlar substrate.

**Fabrication basis:** Laser-induced graphene demonstrates potential in temperature and strain sensing, optical absorption control, and radiation shielding capability on flexible textile substrates for spacesuit applications, with thermal diffusivity in the in-plane direction reaching 2.554 mm²/s — over 9× that of raw Kevlar. [Wiley Online Library](https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/adfm.202411257)

**Sensor node specification:**

- 3-lead dry ECG electrodes: LIG traces, contact impedance ≤ 10 kΩ, bandwidth DC–150 Hz
- Respiratory inductance belt: piezoelectric PVDF fiber woven into the thoracic band, sensitivity ≥ 50 mV/% circumference change
- Galvanic skin response (paw pad sensors): Ag/AgCl electrodes with hydrogel backing, frequency 0.05–1 Hz
- Triaxial IMU nodes (×4, at spine T3, L3, tail base, skull): MEMS accelerometer + gyroscope, range ±16g / ±2000 dps, sampling rate 200 Hz
- Skin temperature: NTC thermistor array (×6), accuracy ±0.1°C
- Radiation dosimeter: PIN diode spectrometer, dose range 0.01–100 mGy/h

**Power consumption per sensor node:** ≤ 5 mW. Total sensor array: ≤ 40 mW.

### 3.5 Layer 4 — Micro-Pressure Stabilization Mesh

In microgravity, venous blood pooling toward the cranial end and fluid shift away from the lower limbs occurs in humans within hours. We extend this model to felines with the following cardiovascular pressure compensation specification.

**Target counter-pressure:** 15–25 mmHg on the thoracic and abdominal walls, delivered by distributed pneumatic micro-bladders (diameter 12 mm each, spacing 25 mm, total ≈ 48 bladders over the torso).

**Pressure delivery system:** Micro-compressor (mass ≤ 15 g, P ≤ 30 mW), regulated by FCAMS heart rate variability monitoring. When HRV-derived vascular tone index drops below threshold θ_vascular = 0.35, bladder pressure increases by ΔP = 5 mmHg.

### 3.6 Layer 5 — Skin-Contact Hypoallergenic Inner Lining

**Material:** Chitosan-silk fibroin composite with embedded slow-release synthetic feline facial pheromone (F3 fraction) microspheres.

**Specification:**
- Fiber diameter: 5–15 μm (comparable to natural fur diameter: 30–100 μm — fine enough to avoid mechanical irritation)
- Breathability: water vapor transmission rate (WVTR) ≥ 1500 g/(m²·day)
- Pheromone release rate: 0.1–0.5 μg F3/hour (within the effective concentration range of synthetic Feliway analogs)
- Antimicrobial: Chitosan's intrinsic cationic charge disrupts gram-positive bacterial cell walls; inhibition zone ≥ 12 mm for *S. aureus* (standard chitosan benchmark)

**Claw accommodation:** Sacrificial dorsal panels of high-tenacity Dyneema fiber, designed as replaceable modules. The scratching behavior is accommodated as a stress-relieving stereotypy rather than suppressed.

