## CHAPTER 6 — BIOETHICS AND FELINE WELFARE FRAMEWORK

### 6.1 Regulatory Landscape

Animals have been integral to space research for decades, yet no international laws specifically protect their welfare during or after missions — and despite growing awareness, the legal and ethical frameworks surrounding animals in space remain underdeveloped. [Phys.org](https://phys.org/news/2025-08-weve-animals-space-decades.html)

COSPAR enacted guidelines requiring ethical approval for all vertebrate animal research in flight, and monitoring animal welfare in a microgravity environment must rely heavily on behavior since many visual markers such as coat condition and temperature are affected in space. [PubMed Central](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11845785/)

The CosmoCat program establishes a compliance pathway that goes beyond minimum COSPAR requirements. Academic literature identifies four legitimate rationales for including animals in space settlement: food resource, research subjects, companionship, and habitat preservation for endangered species [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0016328724001277) — the CosmoCat project maps explicitly onto the companionship rationale.

### 6.2 The Feline Welfare Framework — Ten Principles

**Principle 1 — Sentient Participant.** The cat is a crew member with intrinsic value, not an instrument. Its interests carry independent moral weight.

**Principle 2 — Voluntary Participation.** Suit donning must be achievable via positive reinforcement alone. No physical coercion. The suit must be removable by the cat in any safe habitat location.

**Principle 3 — COSPAR Compliance.** All experimental protocols require IACUC review prior to each mission phase.

**Principle 4 — FWI Monitoring.** The Feline Welfare Index is computed continuously and logged. Any mission phase with FWI < 0.50 requires documented justification.

**Principle 5 — Analog First.** No feline proceeds to orbital deployment without completing a 6-month ground analog trial maintaining FWI ≥ 0.60.

**Principle 6 — Veterinary Authority.** The mission veterinary officer holds veto authority over any mission activity affecting the cat's welfare, independent of mission commander.

**Principle 7 — End-of-Life Protocol.** Humane euthanasia criteria are defined before launch. The cat shall not be subjected to painful procedures without anesthesia.

**Principle 8 — Microbiome Protection.** The cat's habitat zone is physically separated from the primary crew food production area. Housing for animal subjects in space requires specialized hardware that is ventilated but enclosed to contain allergens and other biohazards. [Consensus](https://consensus.app/questions/animal-testing-in-space-exploration/)

**Principle 9 — No Genetic Modification.** The COSMOCAT program does not propose or support genetic modification of the animal for space adaptation in this generation. The suit provides the adaptation layer; the animal remains unmodified.

**Principle 10 — Transparent Reporting.** All FWI data from each mission phase is published in full. No data suppression is permissible.

