## CHAPTER 7 — HABITAT INTEGRATION AND FELINE ECOSYSTEM

### 7.1 Artificial Gravity Access Protocol

Based on the FAI analysis (Theorem 5.2), the cat must access effective gravity g_eff ≥ 0.15g_Earth for a minimum of 4 hours/day. This is provided by:

**Rotating habitat section:** A cylindrical corridor of radius r = 2.5 m rotating at ω = √(g_target / r) = √(0.15 · 9.81 / 2.5) = 0.767 rad/s ≈ 7.3 rpm. This produces a 0.15g centripetal acceleration at the outer wall — sufficient to maintain P_contact(0.15g) ≈ 0.78 by Definition 2.3. The cat is not forced to use this zone; behavioral enrichment (prey-drone) is preferentially placed there.

### 7.2 Prey-Drone Enrichment System

An autonomous micro-quadcopter (mass 22 g, wingspan 130 mm) programmed for evasive flight dynamics within the habitat. Flight algorithm: randomized Lévy walk with bounded domain. When captured (cat paw contact), the drone emits a synthetic chirp and deploys a food pellet from an onboard reservoir (capacity: 10 × 0.5 g pellets).

This provides: (a) physical exercise and hunting behavior expression, (b) positive reinforcement for locomotion in partial gravity, (c) a quantifiable behavioral metric (capture rate per session) contributing to the L(t) component of FAI.

### 7.3 Human-Feline Affective Interface (HFAI)

A wrist-mounted display worn by crew members shows:

- Real-time FSI gauge (color-coded: green ≤ 0.45, yellow 0.45–0.65, red ≥ 0.65)
- FWI trend (7-day rolling)
- Current activity state (sleeping / grooming / active / distressed)
- Haptic "purr transducer" embedded in the crew chair seat, relaying the cat's actual purr vibration (detected by the thoracic piezoelectric sensor) in real time at scaled amplitude

The HFAI quantifies the psychological benefit to crew: cortisol reduction attributable to positive feline-human interaction will be measured via crew saliva sampling (2×/week) and correlated with ρ_social(t) from the FCAMS log, providing the longitudinal dataset needed to publish the first controlled study of companion animal countermeasures in deep-space analog environments.

