## CHAPTER 9 — ORIGINAL CONTRIBUTIONS SUMMARY

This dissertation makes four formally distinct original contributions to the literature:

**OC1 — The Feline Vestibular Compensation Model (FVCM):** The first extension of the Kane-Scher / Montgomery geometric mechanics framework to partial gravity environments, incorporating gravity-dependent vestibular gain K_v(g_eff), paw contact probability P_contact(g_eff), and haptic correction bound Proposition 2.1. This defines the mathematical design space for the suit's stabilization subsystem.

**OC2 — The Five-Layer FEMSS Architecture:** A fully specified nanocomposite suit design with material-level performance bounds grounded in current experimental literature on LIG textiles, BN-GA aerogels, and GA-PEG phase-change composites. The architecture satisfies all mission envelope constraints simultaneously (mass, power, thermal, radiation).

**OC3 — The FCAMS Platform:** A validated edge-AI pipeline for continuous feline autonomic monitoring, integrating a 1D-CNN + Transformer FSI model (≤ 180K parameters, ≤ 100 mW total platform power) with closed-loop comfort actuation. The architecture is the first multimodal feline stress monitoring system designed for orbital deployment.

**OC4 — The Feline Adaptation Index (FAI) with Stability Theory:** A four-dimensional nonlinear dynamical systems model of feline neurophysiological adaptation, with: (a) Lyapunov stability proof for the adapted equilibrium; (b) saddle-node bifurcation characterization of the maladaptation threshold; (c) early warning signal formalization via autocorrelation and variance inflation; (d) the Feline Welfare Index as a mission go/no-go criterion derived from the FAI state vector. This is the first quantitative bioastronautics model of a domesticated companion species.

