## CLOSING STATEMENT

The question this dissertation set out to answer was not whether cats belong in space. That is a question for mission planners, ethicists, and crews who will live with the answer for years. The question was: *if they go, what does responsible science demand?*

It demands a model of how they orient — and we built one from Kane, Scher, and Montgomery. It demands materials that protect without constraining — and we specified them layer by layer. It demands a monitoring system that listens to a creature that cannot speak — and we designed one around its whiskers, its heartbeat, and its gait. And it demands a theory of when adaptation fails — and we proved it, named its threshold, and gave mission operators time to act before it is reached.

Félicette flew in 1963 with electrodes in her brain and no welfare protocol. If a cat next goes to space — and one likely will, in some form, before this century ends — it will go with a suit, a welfare index, a stability proof, and a framework that treats its life as something more than a data point.

That is what the mathematics is for.

